<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cassiodorus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 485 – c. 585</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cassiodorus was a Roman statesman and writer who served the Ostrogothic kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, holding high offices including praetorian prefect. His Variae preserve official letters of the Gothic court, and his lost History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> was summarised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>. In retirement he founded the monastery of Vivarium in southern Italy, where he promoted the copying of Christian and classical texts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassiodorus.jpg" alt="Cassiodorus" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fulda workshop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gesta_Theodorici_-_Flavius_Magnus_Aurelius_Cassiodorus_(c_485_-_c_580).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
